WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
A slime guard watches the hall.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:05.333
It sees everything inside this cone.

3
00:00:05.333 --> 00:00:08.533
Inko sneaks past behind it,
then steps in front.

4
00:00:08.533 --> 00:00:12.467
Spotted! But how does the guard's
code know Inko is in front of it,

5
00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:14.333
and not behind?

6
00:00:14.333 --> 00:00:17.500
One small operation: the dot product.

7
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:21.467
Then we'll meet its 3D partner,
the cross product.

8
00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:27.233
The dot product takes two vectors and
gives back one number, not a vector.

9
00:00:27.233 --> 00:00:30.333
Multiply the matching parts, then add.

10
00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:33.533
Say a is the vector (3, 1).

11
00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:36.800
And b is the vector (2, 2).

12
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:42.700
Three times two is six, one times two
is two, and six plus two is eight.

13
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.000
In 3D, add one more term: z times z.

14
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.033
Three multiplies and two adds,
that's the whole function.

15
00:00:51.033 --> 00:00:55.733
And the order doesn't
matter: a·b equals b·a.

16
00:00:57.100 --> 00:00:59.600
So what does that eight mean?

17
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.067
The same number has a second formula:

18
00:01:02.067 --> 00:01:08.033
|a| |b| cos θ, where theta is the

19
00:01:08.033 --> 00:01:13.400
angle between them. Normalize both
vectors first, and both lengths are one,

20
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.000
so the dot product is just
the cosine of the angle.

21
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.900
Now swing b around a.

22
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.533
Pointing the same way,
the dot is positive.

23
00:01:21.533 --> 00:01:25.467
At ninety degrees it's
exactly zero: perpendicular.

24
00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:27.833
Past ninety, it turns negative.

25
00:01:27.833 --> 00:01:30.933
Front, side or behind: just read the sign.

26
00:01:30.933 --> 00:01:34.033
No angle, and no inverse cosine needed.

27
00:01:35.133 --> 00:01:39.333
Back to the guard. It
faces along a direction, f.

28
00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:44.200
The arrow to Inko is player − guard,
straight from lesson two.

29
00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.400
Normalize both, and their dot
is the cosine of the angle

30
00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.367
between where the guard
looks and where Inko stands.

31
00:01:50.367 --> 00:01:54.100
The cone is sixty degrees wide,
thirty on each side.

32
00:01:54.100 --> 00:02:00.100
So Inko is seen when that dot is
greater than cos 30°, about 0.87.

33
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:05.767
Now the trap: skip the normalize,
and a faraway Inko makes a huge dot,

34
00:02:05.767 --> 00:02:07.867
so it's seen even outside the cone,

35
00:02:07.867 --> 00:02:11.700
while a close Inko,
right in front, doesn't count.

36
00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:15.667
Normalize first,
and only the angle matters.

37
00:02:16.533 --> 00:02:18.633
The dot has one more meaning.

38
00:02:18.633 --> 00:02:22.133
Drop a line from the tip of a
straight onto b's direction.

39
00:02:22.133 --> 00:02:28.133
How far along it lands is a's shadow:
a·b̂, with b normalized.

40
00:02:28.333 --> 00:02:32.033
In the game, dot Inko's velocity
with the direction toward the guard,

41
00:02:32.033 --> 00:02:34.900
and you get how fast Inko is closing in.

42
00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:37.100
Negative means moving away.

43
00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:42.433
Make b longer, and the shadow stays put:
only its direction counts.

44
00:02:43.433 --> 00:02:45.333
Now step into 3D.

45
00:02:45.333 --> 00:02:48.600
The cross product takes two
vectors and gives back a vector,

46
00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.933
one that's perpendicular to both.

47
00:02:50.933 --> 00:02:55.633
But two directions are
perpendicular to both: up and down.

48
00:02:55.633 --> 00:03:00.200
The right-hand rule picks one: curl your
right hand's fingers from a toward b,

49
00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:02.667
and your thumb points along a×b.

50
00:03:02.667 --> 00:03:06.700
A classic check: x cross y gives z.

51
00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:09.000
Swap the order, and the result flips:

52
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.067
b×a is minus a×b.

53
00:03:12.067 --> 00:03:15.300
Unlike the dot product,
order matters here.

54
00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:18.100
Where does graphics use it?

55
00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:22.333
Every triangle needs a normal:
the direction its face points.

56
00:03:22.333 --> 00:03:25.633
Take its corners, A, B and C.

57
00:03:25.633 --> 00:03:30.033
Build two edges from A, B − A and C − A.

58
00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:34.233
Cross them, then normalize:
that's the normal.

59
00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.933
Bonus: the cross product's length is

60
00:03:36.933 --> 00:03:39.767
the area of the parallelogram
the two edges make,

61
00:03:39.767 --> 00:03:42.167
and the triangle is half of it.

62
00:03:42.167 --> 00:03:47.500
Parallel edges give zero, and normalizing
zero is lesson two's NaN trap.

63
00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:49.733
The corner order matters too.

64
00:03:49.733 --> 00:03:53.900
In WebGL and three.js,
corners going counter-clockwise,

65
00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.967
as the camera sees them,
make the front face.

66
00:03:56.967 --> 00:04:00.567
Swap two corners, and the
normal points into the mesh.

67
00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.000
Now both products work together.

68
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.733
Here's a ball and a light.

69
00:04:05.733 --> 00:04:10.767
At each point, take the normal, N,
and the direction toward the light, L,

70
00:04:10.767 --> 00:04:15.967
both normalized. The
brightness is max(0, N·L).

71
00:04:15.967 --> 00:04:19.067
Facing the light: one, fully lit.

72
00:04:19.067 --> 00:04:21.400
Edge-on: zero.

73
00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:23.067
Turned away, the dot goes negative,

74
00:04:23.067 --> 00:04:27.300
and max clamps it to zero,
because light can't be negative.

75
00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.233
The same sign check, against
the camera instead of the light,

76
00:04:30.233 --> 00:04:33.600
lets the GPU skip
triangles that face away.

77
00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.033
That's backface culling.

78
00:04:37.233 --> 00:04:41.867
So: the dot product multiplies
and adds, giving one number.

79
00:04:41.867 --> 00:04:44.533
Its sign says front, side or behind,

80
00:04:44.533 --> 00:04:48.100
and for unit vectors,
it's the cosine of the angle.

81
00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:51.100
The cross product gives a
vector perpendicular to both,

82
00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:53.033
and the order sets which way.

83
00:04:53.033 --> 00:04:57.167
Edges, cross, normalize:
a triangle's normal.

84
00:04:57.167 --> 00:05:00.933
Normal dot light: a lit surface.

85
00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:05.667
Now open the Dot Product and Projection
demo in this lesson, stretch b,

86
00:05:05.667 --> 00:05:10.033
and watch the shadow stay
still while a·b keeps growing.

87
00:05:10.867 --> 00:05:12.434
If this clicked, give it a like,

88
00:05:12.434 --> 00:05:15.200
and subscribe so you don't
miss the next lesson.

89
00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:16.834
See you there!
